  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Northeast winds Friday night through Saturday will create\n   choppy conditions on area lakes.\n\n-  A cutoff low pressure system brings widespread rain, with snow\n   above 6,000 feet Saturday night through Monday morning.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms will continue over western MT through\nnoon, these showers are powered by Jet Stream energy aloft.\nAfternoon showers will continue but mainly in the mountains east\nof US-93, orographic uplift will become the main instigator. \n\nFriday evening an easterly gradient develops over the region and \nwinds slowly turn east northeasterly. Generally, gusts up to 30 \nmph in the gaps should be the strongest winds. For boaters going \nout early Saturday prepare for choppy conditions on area lakes. \n\nThis is a cooler air mass, Thursday was the warmest day with both\nFriday and Saturday 8 to 12 degrees cooler. Then as the closed low\nmoves over the region Sunday, under the core of low pressure temperatures\nmay cool an additional 10 to 15 degrees. Snow levels that are \n8000-9000 feet on Saturday, will lower to around 7000 feet on \nSunday. Historically under the core of low pressure snow levels \nare an additional 1000 feet lower, to around 6000 feet on Sunday\nevening, due to dynamic cooling processes. Still it is September \nand the main message for snow is the same for rain. Sunday into \nMonday in the back country will be wet and raw, unprepared outdoor\nenthusiasts may encounter hypothermia inducing conditions. \n\nThe models have continued a northward trend in the core of best\nprecipitation, still I-90 and northward including all of Idaho and\nClearwater Counties ID, have a 80 to 90% chance of widespread 0.25\nor more precipitation. South and west to Butte, that chance lowers\nto 30 to 40%. Closed lows are notorious for wobbling at the last \nminute, so if total precipitation is important to your activities \nstay abreast of future forecasts. Up to 1 inch in the highest \nterrain of Idaho and Clearwater Counties is still possible, along\nwith the Mission and Seeley Swan Mountains.",
